<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somalis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Somali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Somalis are a Cushitic-speaking people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, predominantly Muslim. They formed an important part of the forces and population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>. Accounts of the Adal Sultanate note their role in its economy and military campaigns.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultanate's pastoral hinterland, the Ogaden and surrounding lowlands, provided livestock for export and was the recruiting ground for its military contingents, drawn predominantly from <b>Somali</b> and Afar populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ajuran Sultanate arose among the <b>Somali</b> clans of the inter-riverine region of southern Somalia, the fertile land between the Shabelle and Jubba rivers, and on the arid plains that stretch north from it towards the Indian Ocean coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Liberation Front fought for Eritrean independence, rebels in Tigray included the nascent Tigray People's Liberation Front, and the Western <b>Somali</b> Liberation Front had by June 1977 driven the army out of much of the Ogaden into fortified towns before Somalia launched a full-scale invasion in the Ogaden War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…first millennium brought larger settlements and greater social complexity, and from the eleventh century the Swahili of the coast acted as middlemen for <b>Somali</b>, Egyptian, Nubian, Arab, Persian and Indian traders, built walled towns and coral houses, and saw their elites convert to Islam and often speak Arabic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>British Somaliland, a protectorate on the Gulf of Aden coast since the 1880s, became independent as the State of Somaliland on 26 June 1960 and five days later, on 1 July, united with the former Italian trust territory of Somalia to form the <b>Somali</b> Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Zeila's two-mihrab mosque, the Masjid al-Qiblatayn, dates from about the seventh century and is among the oldest in Africa; in the late ninth century al-Yaqubi described Muslims living along the northern <b>Somali</b> seaboard and Zeila as a self-governing trading port whose merchants shipped goods to Yemen and Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
